---
import ResourceLayout from '../../components/ResourceLayout.astro';
import VideoEmbed from '../../components/VideoEmbed.astro';
import InfographicViewer from '../../components/InfographicViewer.astro';
import ExternalResource from '../../components/ExternalResource.astro';
---

<ResourceLayout
  icon="❤️"
  eyebrow="Heart health and prevention"
  title="Heart Health"
  introduction="Learn about blood pressure, cholesterol, heart disease prevention, warning signs, and when to seek urgent medical care."
>
  <VideoEmbed
    title="Heart Health for Newcomers"
    videoId="HGUxsClKZ00"
    description="Watch this short video to learn about heart health, cardiovascular risk factors, common warning signs, and ways to protect your heart."
  />

  <InfographicViewer
    title="The ABCDEs of Diabetes and Heart Care"
    description="Learn practical steps for reducing cardiovascular risk, including blood sugar control, blood pressure, cholesterol, healthy habits, and medication use."
    previewSrc="/infographics/mental-health/ABCDEs-of-Diabetes-Heart-Care-en.png"
    previewAlt="Preview of the ABCDEs of diabetes and heart care infographic"
  languages={[
  {
    label: 'English',
    href: '/infographics/mental-health/heart-health-en.png',
    lang: 'en',
  },
  {
    label: 'ਪੰਜਾਬੀ',
    href: '/infographics/mental-health/heart-health-pa.png',
    lang: 'pa',
  },
]}
  />

  <ExternalResource
    icon="🇨🇦"
    source="HealthLink BC"
    title="Heart Health"
    description="Explore trusted information about heart disease, blood pressure, cholesterol, healthy living, and when to seek medical attention."
    href="https://www.healthlinkbc.ca/health-topics/heart-and-circulation"
  />

  <section>
    <h2>Why is heart health important?</h2>
    <p>
      Your heart and blood vessels carry oxygen and nutrients throughout your
      body. High blood pressure, high cholesterol, diabetes, smoking, and other
      risk factors can damage blood vessels over time and increase the risk of
      heart attack and stroke.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Blood pressure</h2>
    <p>
      Blood pressure measures the force of blood against the walls of your
      arteries. High blood pressure often causes no symptoms, so regular checks
      are important. A healthcare provider can explain your results and whether
      lifestyle changes or medication may be needed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Cholesterol</h2>
    <p>
      Cholesterol is a type of fat carried in the blood. Some cholesterol is
      needed by the body, but high levels of LDL cholesterol can contribute to
      plaque buildup in the arteries. Blood tests can help assess your risk.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How can I reduce my risk?</h2>
    <p>
      Regular physical activity, balanced nutrition, avoiding tobacco, limiting
      alcohol, getting enough sleep, managing stress, and taking prescribed
      medications can all help reduce cardiovascular risk.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What tests might I have?</h2>
    <p>
      Common heart-related tests include blood pressure measurements,
      cholesterol and blood sugar tests, an electrocardiogram or ECG, and other
      tests depending on your symptoms and medical history.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Preparing for a heart health appointment</h2>
    <p>
      Bring a list of your medications, write down your symptoms and questions,
      and tell your healthcare provider about any family history of heart
      disease, stroke, high blood pressure, diabetes, or high cholesterol.
    </p>
  </section>

  <aside class="notice">
    <strong>Know the warning signs.</strong>
    <p>
      Call 911 immediately for severe chest pressure, trouble breathing,
      sudden weakness or numbness on one side, facial drooping, sudden trouble
      speaking, fainting, or other symptoms that may indicate a heart attack or
      stroke.
    </p>
  </aside>
</ResourceLayout>